# A session's leavings are named here, not matched by extension. `*.txt` used to sit at this point,
# and its reach was the problem rather than its purpose: it held `AGENTS.md.txt` — a copy of a file
# this package owns — and `.agents/sample.machine.local.json.txt`, another package's machine record,
# out of `git status` and out of both path readings, because ignoring a file is also how a file is
# kept out of a commit without being kept out of the tree. An ignore rule that answers a guard is
# the quiet half of the failure AGENTS.md section 0 describes: the guards read the set a commit
# would carry, so a file this rule hides is one neither of them can report. The names below are what
# a session actually leaves here, so a scratch copy of a file this package owns shows in `git status`
# and a machine record sitting in the carried set is reported by
# `tests/Unit/Support/MachinePathsTest.php` — a sentence rather than a silence, and the colour this
# repository decided on for a file that is somebody else's. The one that arrived that way, the record
# for the package next door, was deleted on 2026-10-27; the paragraph above `/.quarantine/` carries
# that account. `tests/Support/Ignored.php` reads whatever these rules do hold, so a rule that starts
# hiding a record is named rather than trusted.
composer[0-9].json
/composer.phar
/hs_err_pid*.log
/permissions-01.md
/oc-session-*.md

# A file that is not this checkout's to commit or delete has somewhere to be: it goes in here rather
# than outdoors, because a write to a path outside this root is the one edit AGENTS.md section 0
# refuses, and a move rather than a rule over its name is what the paragraph above was written
# against — the guard has to stop reading the file, and nothing else has to be told to look away.
#
# The three files that were here are resolved. The two session configs a workspace agent wrote
# (`opencode.jsonc`, `opencode.v1-backup.jsonc`) turned out to be this checkout's own editor setup,
# which it decided to carry: they are tracked templates now — committed as `63a2465`, and rendered
# into the ignored configs further down by `python manage-config.py`. The machine record for the
# package next door was deleted on 2026-10-27, because a copy of another checkout's record names a
# branch, a head, a tag and a signing key that only that checkout can keep true — nothing here could
# tell whether it was still right — and it was the one entry every run printed a `[NOTE] Ignored:`
# line about. What is left is this directory's own README, which says what was held, what became of
# each file, and what the directory is for now.
#
# Nothing inside is tracked: an index entry is listed by `git ls-files --cached` whatever this file
# says, which is why the two opencode configs were unstaged while they were here.
/.quarantine/

# The editor's agent writes this the first time a checkout is opened as a workspace, and what
# it holds is a project id: local to one machine and belonging to no version of this package.
/.freebuff/

# The workspace configs this checkout's editor reads. They are generated - each `.template` beside
# them is the tracked artifact and `python manage-config.py` writes the config from it, substituting
# the machine-local values the template names as tokens - so the rendered files are this machine's
# and belong in no commit. Ignoring them is what keeps a machine path in them out of `git status`
# and out of both path readings, which read the files a commit would carry.
/opencode.jsonc
/opencode.v1-backup.jsonc

# Machine-local settings. `.agents/machine.local.json` is the one file that names this machine's
# PHP, PowerShell and package directory; each settings file below is rendered from the committed
# `.example` template beside it by `python .agents/render_local.py`. Committing either one would
# put a drive path in the repository, which is what the two path guards exist to refuse.
/.agents/machine.local.json
/.trae/settings.json
/.trae/settings.local.json
/.kiro/settings/local.json

# The checks in `.agents/` are Python, and running one leaves the byte-code of the file it read
# beside that file. It is not a source and not a fact about a machine — it is the one artefact
# these checks produce themselves, and it is ignored rather than committed because the next run
# writes it again.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
